<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#linear-a">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Linear A</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1800–1450 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Linear A is the undeciphered script used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> for administration and religious inscriptions from about 1800 to 1450 BCE. Written on clay tablets, sealings and stone vessels, it records an unknown language. Many of its signs were adapted by the Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> to create <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, so some sound values can be estimated, but the texts cannot be read.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/linear-a.jpg" alt="Linear A" width="320" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Evans, Arthur, Sir (1851-1941) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_A_cup.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
